A||||7||Fformula88||Fluctuating idle on V6||||8||
Z||000000||Fformula88||04-06-2000||05:42 PM||||HELP!!!  I have a 1988 Formula.  The engine will fluctuate on idle SOMETIMES... but not always.  For example, I drove about 75 miles yesterday without any problems.  Today, it stalled everytime I stopped.  It idles up and down, nearly choking itself out in park, and will stall when put into gear.  When driving, it runs without any problems, and then will idle badly at stoplights, and stall.  This erratic problem can disappear for days... and does it in all conditions... when its wet or dry, warm or cold, and even when the engine is warm or cold.  I have had the car to many different mechanics and even two Pontiac dealers.  Here is what everyone did....<P>New plugs, wires, cap/rotor, coil, charcoal canister filter, air filter, EGR tube, new vacumn lines<P>There are no stored codes in the ECM.  If anyone has an idea of what it could be, please pass it along.  I have been reasured by the Pontiac dealer that there are no vacumn leaks... they looked it over for a long time, replaced lines etc.    Thanx for your help!||||reg||8
Z||000001||Phil||04-06-2000||06:37 PM||||Lock up solenoid? unplug it for a few days and see what happens.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Mark||04-06-2000||07:48 PM||||Try cleaning the throttle body, particularly the idle air bypass path.  If it's gunked up, the IAC motor could be slow to react to position change signals, which could lead to stalling.  <P>I've not done this on a Fiero yet, but on my Dodge Caravan, I twice solved stalling problems with a good throttle body cleaning.  It's pretty easy too. I used a common throttle body/carb (aerosol spray) cleaner.  I didn't need to completely disconnect and remove the throttle body either.  I just unbolted it and pulled it away from the intake manifold far enough to get spray the cleaner in from both sides.  You should also catch the drips and runoff with some towels.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Ed||04-06-2000||09:01 PM||||ALERT!!<P>When you squirt the cleaner into the inlet BE SURE the little extension tube on the spray can is FIRMLY attached.  We had one member shoot the plastic tube into the intake of the engine (V-6) where it COULD NOT be retreived. The good news is he let it melt in there and nothing bad happened.  <P>It even happened to me and I knew better.  I was able to retrieve the tube, however.<P>Watch out! ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Old Lar||04-06-2000||09:18 PM||||I have the same problem with my V6. The engine service light would come on, but not stay on after the ignition was shut off and restared.  It has been suggested that it could be the EGR pipe to the TPS (throttle position sensor).  It leaves a code 32.  The EGR valve was replace last year.  That was an easy fix.  <P>The Haynes manual suggests vacuum leak, plugged PCV, air filter clogged, leaking head gasket, timing chain or camshaft worn.  For me I probably have a vacuum leak some where.  The car drove to Florida and back getting 30+ mpg.  Only when I stopped was there a problem.||||reg||9
Z||000005||Luperman||04-06-2000||10:10 PM||||guys I had the same problem and it frustrated me years and recently during my bucking and surging problem still ongoing by the way , so any way I removed the IAC valve and I could not believe how much carbon was built up on the tip of it not allowing it to open and close properly so I cleaned it and the mating surface and wow I have a great idling car once again , it's nice when you go to put it in park it stays running . hope this helps good luck...||||reg||6
Z||000006||Fformula88||04-06-2000||10:39 PM||||I think I'll take a look at the IAC.  That is somewhere I haven't looked yet.  I have tried cleaning the throttle body... didn't help.  I even took the whole intake plenum apart and cleaned out all the passages.  It didn't really help either.<P>I was getting the EGR code, and replaced the valve, which looked like it needed it but didn't help.  Then the EGR tube, which turned out the light.  It was leaking air, but didn't fix the overall problem.||||reg||1
Z||000007||wkayl||04-07-2000||02:15 AM||||My 86 v6 had an eratic idle.  The other day I rebuilt the starter and when I did I also checked the throttle pos sensor.  It was way off.  I adjusted it (do a search in the forum for procedure to adjust).  I don't know if it was resetting the ECM when I disconnected the batt or adjusting the TPS, but it seem to idle better.  It was only yesterday I did this so the 600 mile trip I'm going to make Sun evening and Mon will be the test. ||||reg||1
